      "mode": "fresh",
      "prompt": "STYLE: \"her notebook games\". Everything is drawn by a 14-year-old in her Scribble Defender notebook: confident blue ballpoint pen outlines (thick, slightly uneven, some double strokes) with dense colored-pencil fill and visible pencil strokes. Bold, readable silhouettes that still read when shrunk small. Never glossy, never vector, never 3D, never photo-real. Match the hand of the attached approved title art and notebook cover.\n\nThree separate extremely long, very thin horizontal strips of rolling-hill ground for a side-scrolling game, stacked one above the other with wide gaps between them, on a perfectly flat, uniform pure magenta background (#FF00FF). Each strip is a solid rectangular band of grass and earth seen from the side, exactly about 16 times wider than tall (thin like a ribbon), running the full width of the image. Its TOP edge is a perfectly straight horizontal edge: the green colored-pencil grass fill is cut off flat along it like a ruler edge, solid all the way up to that edge with no magenta showing below it anywhere, and one thin dark green ballpoint stroke drawn right on that edge, touching the grass. Nothing at all rises above the top edge: no tufts, flowers or blades poke above it. Below the edge: dense green colored-pencil grass with short blades drawn inside the band, fading through darker green into brown earth with a few pebbles and roots; the bottom edge dissolves into loose pencil strokes. Strip 1: spring green with a few tiny white daisies. Strip 2: deeper summer green with small clover patches. Strip 3: yellow-green late-summer grass with a few dry tufts. Each strip is uniform along its length, with no single big feature, so it can repeat side by side. No sky, no objects, no text. SCALE: this ground sits under tiny game characters only as tall as the strip itself, so draw every detail very small and fine: grass blades are short fine hatching strokes no taller than one tenth of the strip height, pebbles are tiny ovals 